            <div xml:id="d146">

                <pb facs="0245" n="147" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg147"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/1265</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-07-16">July 16, 1930</date>.<lb/>[Received August
                        25.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 326</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith, for
                    the Department’s information, a copy of a letter, with its enclosures, dated
                    Tsingtao, July 1, 1930, from Admiral Charles B. McVay, Jr.,<anchor n="59"
                        xml:id="d146-f1"/> Commander-in-Chief of the United States Asiatic Fleet, in
                    regard to the assistance rendered Americans by the Commanding Officer of H. B.
                    M. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Cricket</hi> and by the British Consul at Changsha
                    on the occasion of the withdrawal from that city early in June of Central
                    Government forces under the command of General Ho Chien. Likewise enclosed is a
                    copy of my note of appreciation to the British Minister.<anchor n="59"
                        xml:id="d146-f2"/></p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mahlon F.
                            Perkins</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Counselor of
                        Legation</hi></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
